# Said at the one moment it is useful, and it says nothing else.
#
# No package manager on Linux removes files from a home directory — pacman's
# hooks run as root against system paths, as dpkg's and rpm's do — so removing
# Sieve leaves every wallet exactly where it was. That is the right behaviour
# for a wallet rather than an oversight: a reinstall, a repository change or a
# distribution upgrade must never be able to take a sealed seed with it.
#
# Which leaves one problem, and this fixes it: the only person who can clear
# this up is the one reading their terminal after `pacman -R sieve`, and they
# cannot do it without being told where to look.
#
# **This must never delete anything.** PACKAGING.md says so at greater length,
# to the packager who will eventually be tempted to be helpful here. A hook
# that removed ~/.local/share/sieve would destroy coins for anybody who had not
# written their recovery phrase down, during an operation that looks routine.
post_remove() {
	cat <<'EOF'

Your wallets have not been removed.

  wallets      ~/.local/share/sieve
  preferences  ~/.config/sieve

Delete those directories only if you can restore the wallet without them —
your recovery phrase written down, or the hardware device it was imported
from. That is the only other way back to the coins.

EOF
}
